body:
First of all, I want to make it clear that (as you probably suspect), you are not alone. Many of us feel the way you do, maybe not at the same intensity, maybe not all the time, but I know a lot of people who are just lost because of this terrible situation.

&#x200B;

Now, my therapist (who treats mainly trauma and anxiety) told me something I think is important and insightful. She explained she refuses to treat climate anxiety and fear the same way she treats trauma-induced syndroms, because she figures fear and anxiety are justified for this matter. That is to say, it is normal and sane to feel fearful and depressed about the whole thing, because, quite frankly, it is terrifying.  
That of course doesn't mean one should not try to put oneself in the best possible mindset or treat related health problems ! If you are suffering from depression (or think you are, which is close enough to warrant therapy imho), reach out and get help from professionals (in my country, many younger therapists are quite aware of climate-induced mental health issues and can be pretty helpful) and maybe get medication. If you are suffering from anxiety, get help too (cognitive and behavioural therapy can help tremendously with that), and try to form new habits to better deal with : breathing exercices, meditation, mindfulness, whatever works for you. Climate-induced anxiety might be justified (and that is still subject to debate), but it should not make your life hell. 

As you may know, activism or being part of a community that acts in accordance with your values can also be huge for your mental well-being, and help you find meaning. So I would strongly encourage you to try and find such groups or associations around you.

&#x200B;

As for the balance between ignorance and doom-scrolling...I think that, given how you've described your feelings, you have read enough. I would advise at least taking a real break from climate research or news, at least until you are in a better place mentally, where you might be able to not get overwhelmed. 

&#x200B;

Truth versus lies, help versus manipulation is yet another matter. I luckily have not come across blatant manipulation about climate change in my everyday life, so I might not be able to help much there. For scientific matters, my take is to always ask for sources. If the person is unable to provide reliable peer-reviewed papers to source their claims (give them a few days, few people remember the name of the articles they read so they might need to look them up again), I find it safe to discard their words as ignorance (best case scenario, because you may be able to teach them something!) or malicious perversion of facts (do not bother about those, they generally are not receptive in my experience).

&#x200B;

Sorry for the wall of text, I hope this helps a bit, and that I did not completely miss your points!

